From 279d710278774ee7113dfcb4c02d7f39b111307e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitry B <dimitryB@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 22:51:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03/15] eip155: check the payer's signature first, and reject
 one an EOA cannot have made
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

x402's exact scheme on EVM checks the signature before balances and allowances
(scheme_exact_evm.md: EIP-3009 verification step 1; Permit2 verification
step 1, "must execute these checks in order"). x402-rs read the balance (and,
for Permit2, the allowance) first, so an unfunded payer with a bad signature
got insufficient_funds or permit2_allowance_required instead of an invalid
signature.

Worse, StructuredSignature::try_from_bytes treats a 64/65-byte signature that
does not recover to the payer as an EIP-1271 signature, so that smart wallets
work. For an EOA payer (no code) such a signature can never be valid, but it
was passed to the transfer simulation anyway. The simulation reverted
("FiatTokenV2: invalid signature", Permit2 InvalidSigner()) and the revert
surfaced as a transport error: HTTP 500 with unexpected_error, from both
/verify and /settle.

The v2 exact verify and settle paths, for both EIP-3009 and Permit2, now check
the signature before anything else is read from the chain: an EOA signature
must recover to the payer (as before), and an EIP-1271 signature is only
accepted from a payer that has code. This costs one eth_getCode, only on the
EIP-1271 path. EIP-6492 signatures (counterfactual wallets) are still checked
on chain by the validator.

The v2 exact handler reports signature failures with the code x402 v2 §9
defines for the exact scheme on EVM, invalid_exact_evm_payload_signature.
